<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Volume</title>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the region bounded by the graph <m>y=4-x^2</m> and the
  <m>x</m>-axis is rotated about the <m>x</m>-axis.  Determine the
  cross-sectional area of a typical slice and then compute the volume
  of the resulting solid.</p>
      <p><image source="figures/volume-region-2.eps"/></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>One way to obtain a sphere is to spin a circle about one of its
  diameters.  Find the volume of a sphere of radius 2 by taking the
  top half of the circle of radius of 2 centered at the origin and
  spinning it about the <m>x</m>-axis.  You may remember that the upper
  semicircle of radius 2 is the graph <m>y = \sqrt{4-x^2}</m>.</p>
      <p><em>Over</em></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Now we are going to look at the region between the graphs
  <m>y=4-x^2</m> and <m>y=x+2</m>, as shown below, and find the volume of the
  solid that results when this region is spun about the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
      <p>Sketch a typical cross-section, determine its area, and then find
  the volume of the resulting solid.</p>
      <p><image source="figures/volume-region.eps"/></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the graph of the function <m>y=x^2</m> and the region
  bounded by this graph, the line <m>y=4</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis.  Find the
  volume of the solid that results from spinning this region about the
  <m>y</m>-axis.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
